{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[{"sourceType":"datasetVersion","sourceId":19600274}],"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# US County Unemployment Rate Analysis","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"Unemployment is an important indicator of economic conditions and can vary significantly across states, counties, months, and economic periods. This project analyzes U.S. county level unemployment data to understand unemployment patterns over time and identify differences across geographic areas. The analysis includes data cleaning, handling missing and duplicate records, standardizing the dataset, and exploring unemployment trends using Python and Pandas. It examines unemployment rates by state, county, month, year, and recession period, and identifies the highest and lowest unemployment levels, data coverage across states, monthly patterns, and differences in unemployment between counties. The goal of this analysis is to uncover meaningful patterns and provide a clear understanding of how unemployment changed across different regions and economic periods.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"**Data Loading and Cleaning**","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import json\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nimport seaborn as sns\n\nwith open(\"/kaggle/input/datasets/manishamali/us-unployment-rate-analysis/result.json\") as f:\n    data = json.load(f)\n\n# flatten the json, skipping the annual averages\nrows = []\nfor year, months in data.items():\n    for month, states in months.items():\n        if month == \"Annual\":\n            continue\n        for state, metrics in states.items():\n            for county, rate in metrics[\"Unemployment Rate\"].items():\n                rows.append([year, month, state, county, rate])\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=[\"Year\", \"Month\", \"State\", \"County\", \"UnemploymentRate\"])\nprint(\"Initial shape:\", df.shape)\n\nfor col in [\"Month\", \"State\", \"County\"]:\n    df[col] = df[col].str.strip()\n\ndf[\"Year\"] = pd.to_numeric(df[\"Year\"], errors=\"coerce\")\ndf[\"UnemploymentRate\"] = pd.to_numeric(df[\"UnemploymentRate\"], errors=\"coerce\")\n\nprint(\"\\nMissing values:\")\nprint(df.isnull().sum())\ndf = df.dropna()\ndf[\"Year\"] = df[\"Year\"].astype(int)\n\nMONTHS = [\"January\", \"February\", \"March\", \"April\", \"May\", \"June\", \"July\",\n          \"August\", \"September\", \"October\", \"November\", \"December\"]\n\nprint(\"\\nInvalid months:\", (~df[\"Month\"].isin(MONTHS)).sum())\ndf = df[df[\"Month\"].isin(MONTHS)]\n\ndf[\"MonthNum\"] = df[\"Month\"].map({m: i + 1 for i, m in enumerate(MONTHS)})\ndf[\"Date\"] = pd.to_datetime(dict(year=df[\"Year\"], month=df[\"MonthNum\"], day=1))\ndf[\"Month\"] = pd.Categorical(df[\"Month\"], categories=MONTHS, ordered=True)\n\n# rates should be between 0 and 100\nprint(\"Rates out of range:\", (~df[\"UnemploymentRate\"].between(0, 100)).sum())\ndf = df[df[\"UnemploymentRate\"].between(0, 100)]\n\n# very high and zero rates look real (small counties), so keeping them\nprint(\"Min rate:\", df[\"UnemploymentRate\"].min(), \"| Max rate:\", df[\"UnemploymentRate\"].max())\nprint(\"Rates above 50%:\", (df[\"UnemploymentRate\"] > 50).sum())\nprint(\"Rates equal to 0:\", (df[\"UnemploymentRate\"] == 0).sum())\n\n# drop rows that are exactly the same in every column\nprint(\"\\nExact duplicates:\", df.duplicated().sum())\ndf = df.drop_duplicates()\n\n# some county months are not in the file at all, so count them here\nexpected = df.groupby([\"State\", \"County\"]).ngroups * df[\"Date\"].nunique()\nprint(\"Missing county months:\", expected - len(df), \"out of\", expected)\nprint(\"States:\", df[\"State\"].nunique())\n\ndf = df.sort_values([\"Date\", \"State\", \"County\"]).reset_index(drop=True)\nprint(\"\\nFinal shape:\", df.shape)\ndf.head()","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-17T20:00:13.407512Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-17T20:00:13.407903Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-17T20:00:17.793769Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-11-17T20:00:13.407872Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-17T20:00:17.79287Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Initial shape: (885548, 5)\n\nMissing values:\nYear                0\nMonth               0\nState               0\nCounty              0\nUnemploymentRate    0\ndtype: int64\n\nInvalid months: 0\nRates out of range: 0\nMin rate: 0.0 | Max rate: 58.4\nRates above 50%: 8\nRates equal to 0: 17\n\nExact duplicates: 0\nMissing county months: 49192 out of 934740\nStates: 47\n\nFinal shape: (885548, 7)\n","output_type":"stream"},{"execution_count":261,"output_type":"execute_result","data":{"text/plain":"   Year    Month    State          County  UnemploymentRate  MonthNum  \\\n0  1990  January  Alabama  Autauga County               6.4         1   \n1  1990  January  Alabama  Baldwin County               6.2         1   \n2  1990  January  Alabama  Barbour County               7.1         1   \n3  1990  January  Alabama     Bibb County              12.4         1   \n4  1990  January  Alabama   Blount County               5.2         1   \n\n        Date  \n0 1990-01-01  \n1 1990-01-01  \n2 1990-01-01  \n3 1990-01-01  \n4 1990-01-01  ","text/html":"<div>\n<style scoped>\n    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n        vertical-align: middle;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n        vertical-align: top;\n    }\n\n    .dataframe thead th {\n        text-align: right;\n    }\n</style>\n<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n  <thead>\